            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Makes a grid of points covering a rectangle: the x, y and z of every point.\n\nUse it for anything laid out on a regular grid - positions to draw or place things at, points to sample a field or an image at, or the x and y inputs to a calculation over a plane.\n\nTHE OUTPUT IS A 3-D ARRAY:\nshaped (divisions y, divisions x, 3) - rows going down, columns going across, and three numbers in every cell: that point's x, its y and its z. Slice the last axis to get one coordinate as a plane: np.[] :,:,0 gives every point's x, np.[] :,:,1 every point's y.\n\nx runs from min x to max x along each row; y runs from min y to max y down the rows, so row 0 holds min y. Both ends are included, as in np.linspace - the grid is two linspaces crossed. z is the same at every point.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.grid <min x> <max x> <min y> <max y> <divisions x> <divisions y>\nDefaults: 0 1 0 1 10 10.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.grid 0 1 0 1 5 4\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin (button):\nClick it, or send it anything, to send the grid. Changing the properties does not send by itself - in the demo a metro clicks the node 10 times a second, so the planes follow your drags.\n\nmin x / max x / min y / max y (drag floats):\nThe rectangle.\n\ndivisions x / divisions y (drag ints):\nHow many POINTS along each direction (not gaps). Anything below 2 is treated as 2.\n\nz (drag float):\nThe z of every point. Default 0; it has no argument.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ngrid points:\nfloat32, shaped (divisions y, divisions x, 3).\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nnp.shape shows the layout: 4 rows, 5 columns, 3 numbers each. The left heat_map is the x plane - the same across every row, rising to the right. The right one is the y plane - the same along every row, rising row by row. Each cell prints its value while there is room.\n\nThe heat_map draws row 0 at the TOP, so y grows DOWN the picture - the opposite of a graph. Both heat_maps are scaled 0 to 1; drag max x or max y past 1 and the far cells saturate.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.linspace makes one of these axes on its own. np.[] slices out a plane. np.reshape turns the grid into a plain list of points: np.reshape -1 3.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 760,
